To create an automated table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `\tableofcontents` command along with the `tocbibind` package and its configuration options. Here's an example:

1. First, add the following line to the preamble of your LaTeX document to include the `tocbibind` package:

```latex
\usepackage[nottoc]{tocbibind}
```

Here, we've specified the option `nottoc`, which prevents the table of contents from including the table of contents itself.

2. Within the document body, after every section or chapter command, add the corresponding `\addtocontents` and `\protect` commands immediately afterward. For example:

```latex
\chapter{Introduction}
\addtocontents{toc}{\protect\chapter{Introduction}\hfill}

\section{Section One}
\addtocontents{toc}{\protect\subsection{Section One}\hfill}

\subsection{Subsection One}
\addtocontents{toc}{\protect\hspace{1em}\underline{\small Subsection One}\hfill}

\addtocontents{toc}{\protect\vspace{0.5em}} % Add some space between sections/chapters
```

This tells LaTeX to insert each section/sub-section title into the appropriate level of the `toc` file. The `\protect` command ensures that any special characters used within a command won't interfere with the TOC formatting.

3. Finally, add the `\tableofcontents` command anywhere you want the TOC to appear in the document. It will automatically generate based on the entries in the `toc` file created by adding the `\addtocontents` statements:

```latex
\tableofcontents
```

With these steps, the resulting LaTeX document should produce an automated table of contents! Note that this is just one way to create a TOC in LaTeX; there are other packages available as well.

